<!-- this exercise was written by Jennifer Moorhouse as part of the PROTEUS grant-->


    <p>
        For each of the following prompts, drag the various points on the curve to create a graph of a continuous function that has the stated combination of properties.
    </p>
    <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
            <p>Exactly one relative maximum and one relative minimum. </p>

          <setup>
            <point name="P1">(-6,0)</point>
            <point name="P2">(-3,0)</point> 
            <point name="P3">(0,0)</point> 
            <point name="P4">(3,0)</point>
            <point name="P5">(6,0)</point> 
          </setup>
    
<graph size="small" fixAxes>
  <shortDescription>A graph of a curve through four adjustable points</shortDescription>
  $P1 $P2 $P3 $P4 $P5
  <function name="f" through="$P1 $P2 $P3 $P4 $P5"></function>
</graph>
          
<answer><award><when>$f.numMinima=1 and $f.numMaxima=1 </when></award>   </answer>     

  <!--        numMinima calculated on [-100,100] but xscale can change where it is calculated -->
          
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                One relative maximum but no global minimum
            </p>

          <setup>
            <point name="Q1">(-6,0)</point>
            <point name="Q2">(-3,0)</point> 
            <point name="Q3">(0,0)</point> 
            <point name="Q4">(3,0)</point>
            <point name="Q5">(6,0)</point> 
          </setup>
    
<graph size="small" fixAxes>
  <shortDescription>A graph of a curve through four adjustable points</shortDescription>
  $Q1 $Q2 $Q3 $Q4 $Q5
  <function name="g" through="$Q1 $Q2 $Q3 $Q4 $Q5"></function>
</graph>
          
<answer><award><when>$g.numMinima=0 and $g.numMaxima=1 </when></award>   </answer>     

        </li>
       <li>
            <p>
                Two relative maxima but no global minimum
            </p>
           <setup>
            <point name="R1">(-6,0)</point>
            <point name="R2">(-3,0)</point> 
            <point name="R3">(0,0)</point> 
            <point name="R4">(3,0)</point>
            <point name="R5">(6,0)</point> 
          </setup>
    
<graph size="small" fixAxes>
  <shortDescription>A graph of a curve through four adjustable points</shortDescription>
  $R1 $R2 $R3 $R4 $R5
  <function name="h" through="$R1 $R2 $R3 $R4 $R5"></function>
</graph>
          
<answer><award><when> $h.numMaxima=2 </when></award>   </answer>     
  <!--$h.globalMinimum needs to be the empty set... how do we check this?  Is this necessary given the (artifical) constraints of this graph?-->
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                One global minimum but no relative minimum.  
            </p>
                    
           <setup>
            <point name="S1">(-6,0)</point>
            <point name="S2">(-3,0)</point> 
            <point name="S3">(0,0)</point> 
            <point name="S4">(3,0)</point>
            <point name="S5">(6,0)</point> 
          </setup>
    
<graph size="small" fixAxes>
  <shortDescription>A graph of a curve through four adjustable points</shortDescription>
  $S1 $S2 $S3 $S4 $S5
  <function name="k" through="$S1 $S2 $S3 $S4 $S5"></function>
</graph>
          
  <setup>   <text name="blank"/>
  </setup>

  <answer name="STUans" type="text">
    <textInput expanded>
      <label> Explain why such a combination is impossible.</label>
    </textInput>
    <award><when>0=0</when></award>
  </answer>

<feedback condition="$STUans!=$blank"> <p>You might have noticed that every time you create a global minimum, the point at which that global minimum occurs is also a local/relative minimum.</p> </feedback>          
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Two relative maxima but no relative minimum.  
            </p>
                    
           <setup>
            <point name="T1">(-6,0)</point>
            <point name="T2">(-3,0)</point> 
            <point name="T3">(0,0)</point> 
            <point name="T4">(3,0)</point>
            <point name="T5">(6,0)</point> 
          </setup>
    
<graph size="small" fixAxes>
  <shortDescription>A graph of a curve through four adjustable points</shortDescription>
  $T1 $T2 $T3 $T4 $T5
  <function name="M" through="  $T1 $T2 $T3 $T4 $T5"></function>
</graph>
          
  <setup>   <text name="blank"/>
  </setup>

  <answer name="STUans" type="text">
    <textInput expanded>
      <label> Explain why such a combination is impossible.</label>
    </textInput>
    <award><when>0=0</when></award>
  </answer>

<feedback condition="$STUans!=$blank"> <p>You might have noticed that whenever you create two relative maxima, a relative minimum occurs between those two points. The only way to keep this from happening is to "break" the function between the two maxima, for example if there were a vertical asymptote.</p> </feedback>  
 
        </li>
 
    </ol>
